# steps/named-struct-exec.md §12 (Q45): a method's call in a named Structure's body is a statement of the
# body, as in a method's body -- put, declared above, and later, declared below (methods are visible both
# ways), each print what they got.  seen and last are hidden inputs of put and later, and so of Counter: a
# procedure without arguments is one with hidden arguments (the author, 2026-09-28, OPUS-CODEX-20260928-08).
# Each execution from the root hands them the root's 0: `put 5 5`, `later 1 1`, twice -- n's declaration runs
# again, n 1.  Their bare writes change only their activations' values (Q52): the root's seen and last stay 0.
# Entry 7.
size_t: seen 0U
size_t: last 0U
fn: put (size_t: a) int
    seen: seen + a
    c.printf: "put %zu %zu\n" a seen
return: 0
Counter:
    size_t: n 0U
    put: 5U
    n: n + 1U
    later: n
return
fn: later (size_t: v) int
    last: last + v
    c.printf: "later %zu %zu\n" v last
return: 0
Counter
Counter
int: ok 0
if: seen = 0U && Counter\n = 1U && last = 0U
    ok: 7
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
